<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Notifications' cross-section surface, carved into its own leaf project because sections
    from all over the app reach into it — Auth, Campaigns, Camps, Consent, Feedback,
    GoogleIntegration (the section and its contracts leaf both), Governance, Issues,
    Onboarding, Shifts, Teams and Users, plus the Shell for the bell.
    A Contracts/ *folder* inside Humans.Notifications would make each of them reference the
    whole section (design §15.5b, memory/architecture/section-project-cycle-fix.md).

    INotificationRetention has one consumer, CleanupNotificationsJob in
    Humans.Notifications/Jobs/, which is inside the section — so that interface could move
    inward. It stays out here for now; that is a later pass. (The job lives in the section
    because "Hangfire pins a job to its assembly" was re-measured and found false:
    AddOrUpdate<T>(id, …) rewrites the stored type string at every startup, so the job id is
    the stable key.)

    It holds exactly what lives outside the section: the dispatch, auto-resolve and retention
    interfaces, and the enums those signatures name — NotificationSource,
    NotificationClass and NotificationPriority cannot follow the entities into the section's
    internal Domain/ because every emit call site outside the section names them
    (design §15 step 5, Budget's rule). The inbox read models, the meter surface and the
    repository have no consumer outside the section and stay internal.

    References Humans.Base only — the bottom of the graph.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Base\Humans.Base.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
